A||||4||FieroBUZZ||No Start...Where to start?||||1||
Z||000000||FieroBUZZ||12-29-2001||03:47 PM||||History..Started and ran fine (stock 2.8)till<P>Yesterday morning it started first crank (as usual) and I went into the house to fetch something.  When I came out it had stalled and refused to start.  Turns over fine and I can hear the fuel pump run to pressure the system. I have 3 other  2.8's here so any hints as to what I should swap out first to find a cure. (module, coil etc). After repeated attempts to start there is a strong gas smell...can it be flooded? Haven't been able to start it in repeated tries.<P>It really didn't give any prior warning other than being a bit harder to hot start.<BR>TIA<BR>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||12-29-2001||04:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroBUZZ:<BR><B>there is a strong gas smell...can it be flooded?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Think it <B>can</B> flood...  if battery is still strong, floor the accelerator and hold it there, and try cranking again (that's what my owner's manual says anyway).  If no luck, then might try pulling the fuel pump fuse and crank until it fires, then reinstall fuse and restart.<P>If no good, pull a plug to verify spark.  If no spark, could be module or coil - easy to swap with one from your other 2.8's.  You probably already knew most or all of this, but you asked for suggestions.  Those are the things I'd probably try first.  Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||12-29-2001||06:19 PM||||I know when any of our v6's would not start it has always been the ign module. I know the one in the 86 GT was new with a new pick up coil in the late spring/early summer. The 86 se got a new one some time when I owned it, cause I put in a used one that lasted only a few weeks.<P>Hope this help. <P>I would start by checking for spark.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroBUZZ||12-29-2001||07:53 PM||||Pete: It is the GT that wouldn't start and I remembered you said the ignition module was new.  It has performed faultlessly and is a ball to drive.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Hopefully, I get to see what Becky's Caddy is like next summer.<BR>I have been busy and not gotten to troubleshoot (I will tomorrow). <BR>I thought I read somewhere that every time you turn the key to 'on' gas is squirted into the cylinders; ergo flooded. I had the battery on the charger and will attack it in the morning.  I figured if it was flooded, that it was too cold for the fuel to evaporate by itself.<P>Just trying to narrow the search. Thanks guys.<BR>Gary<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hairy_Fiero||12-29-2001||10:09 PM||||TO determine if it is an electrical problem, pull your number one spark plug wire and hold the plug connector just above the plug. Have someone crank the car over when you do this and see if you get a spark. If you don't it's either your ignition module, ignition coil or your ignition pickup coil. Good luck.||||reg||1
